lesson coding-a-block-of-your-own v=2 format=teach grade=4 unit=9 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A block of your own"
  goal "Read a program that names a few blocks as one new block, use it more than once, and count what it saves."

  look {
    scene 30x22 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        blocks b code=["define hooray", "  jump", "  clap 2", "hooray", "spin", "hooray"]
        dance d code=["define hooray", "  jump", "  clap 2", "hooray", "spin", "hooray"]
      }
    }
    say """
      The pink block on top only says what hooray means. After that, hooray is used like any other
      block, and each time the robot jumps and claps twice, like the chorus of a song that is written
      once and sung more than once.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice coding.define-count count=1 seed=2 hard-seed=7
    practice coding.define-repeat count=2 seed=3 hard-seed=7
    practice coding.define-written-out count=2 seed=1
    practice coding.define-reach count=2 seed=4 hard-seed=7
    show coding.define-where a=2 z=3 easy-a=1 easy-z=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.define-change m=3 u=4 easy-m=2 easy-u=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.define-ways s=4 k=2 easy-s=3 easy-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "A block of your own is a name for some blocks. Using the name does them all, and changing it once changes it everywhere."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is a stretch for this age and is fine to take slowly. The idea to take away is that a long
    program gets easier to read when a part of it has a name, and easier to change, because changing
    hooray once changes it everywhere it is used. In the jumps and claps question the hooray after the
    repeat counts too. The three-star question can be done by writing every program as a row of H
    and S and crossing out repeats; there are six.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level hooray claps once or twice, the repeated hooray makes nine claps or fewer, Dan writes the twirl out two or three times, the zigzag reaches a flag two or three rows down and one square on, the robot to place zigzags twice and goes one more right, cheer claps twice in a dance of six claps, and the three-star counts the three programs with one hooray in three lines."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level hooray claps two or three times, the repeated hooray makes ten claps or more, Dan writes the twirl out three or four times, the zigzag reaches a flag two or three rows down and two or three squares on, and the robot to place, the cheer with twelve claps and the three-star's six programs are as written, since each is already the item's largest version."
  }
}
